1 OCCU oilwell tender
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Johan Engman was born in Djupsjö, Nordmaling parish, Västerbotten county, Sweden, the 25th of May 1876. He was the son of the crofter in Djupsjö Jonas Petter Engman (1831-1902) and his wife Vilhelmina Persdotter (1
2 CONC 833-1909). Johan was baptized the 28th of May 1876 in Nordmaling parish of the Church of Sweden ("Svenska Kyrkan", Lutheran).
2 CONT
2 CONT Johan grew up in Djupsjö together with his parents and sibling. The 11th of November 1891 he moved to the Baggård (a village nearby) and started working as a farmhand. He returned to his parents in Djupsjö a year later, the 18th o
2 CONC f November 1892. Next spring, the 29th of March 1893 he emigrated to North America.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1900 Johan lived in Wetmore, McKean county in Pennsylvania. He had learnt to speak English and his first name was changed from Johan to John.  He worked as day laborer. John rented a house in Wetmore and had a boarder,
2 CONC  Erick Engman (born in June 1865 in Sweden. No family ties to John has been found yet). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The 15th of May 1903 John Engman married Lydia Christina Anderson in Smethport, McKean county. Lydia was born 1874 in Lockenvi parish, Kalmar county, Sweden. She had immigrated to the US in 1901.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:  Mabel Elvina (born 1903), John Alvin (born 1906), Carl Arthur (born 1908), Arnold Mauritz (born 1910), Mildred Louella (born 1913) och Herbert Leroy (born 1915).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1910 the Engman family lived at Spruce Avenue in Kane, McKean county. John was working in the lumber industry. Lydia and John became US citizens in 1918.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1920 the Engman family owned a farm in West Wind Road, Wetmore township, McKean county. John was a wage-laborer, a pumper, in the oil industry. His wife Lydia and daughter Mabel were working on the home farm. All the children w
2 CONC ere still living at home.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1930 John and Lydia lived in Jojo Road in Wetmore together with their children Mabel, Arnold, Mildred and Herbert. They didn´t own a radio set. John still worked in the oil industry and was now a oilwell tender. Mabel worked at
2 CONC  a bensol factory and Arnold was a farm worker. John Alvin had married in 1928 and moved out. Carl Arthur had died in Ohio 1929. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1940 the John and Lydia Engman lived on their farm at Road 301 in Wetmore. John continued to work as an oil-well tender. Mabel was still living at home and worked in a wood processing factory. John Alvin was divorced and had re
2 CONC turned home before 1935. He worked as an oil-well tender. Arnold worked on the farm. Herbert was married and lived on the farm together with his wife Rose and their sons Arthur L and Philip D. Herbert also worked as an oil-well te
2 CONC nder.
2 CONT
2 CONT Utdrag ur Släktberättelse över släkten Engman i Djupsjö, Nyåker m.fl. orter utarbetad 1961 av Tyko Lundkvist: Johan f. 1876 var farmare i Kane Pa, USA, g.m. småländskan Lydia f. 1872, d. 1958.
2 CONT
2 CONT oil well tender John Engman living on Road 301 in Wetmore township, McKean county, Pennsylvania in 1940. He was living in the same house the 1st of April 1935.
2 CONT
2 CONT Källor/ Sources: Nordmalings kyrkoarkiv, Födelse- och dopböcker, SE/HLA/1010141/C/6 (1860-1878) p 199, 1876 Nr 96. Nordmalings kyrkoarkiv, Husförhörslängder, SE/HLA/1010141/A I/14a (1873-1880) p 139 Djupsjö. Nordmalings kyrkoarkiv
2 CONC , Husförhörslängder, SE/HLA/1010141/A I/15a (1880-1890) p 148 Djupsjö. Nordmalings kyrkoarkiv, Husförhörslängder, SE/HLA/1010141/A I/16a (1891-1899) p 55 Baggård p 169 Djupsjö. Svenskar i USA 1940 (USA) PA:05 1940 bild 7560 https:
2 CONC //sv.findagrave.com/memorial/83672249/jonas-petter-engman
2 CONT
